# Asymmetric Information and Morality

Asymmetric information—where one party has more knowledge than another—creates moral hazards when the better-informed party faces no accountability for the outcomes of their decisions. Taleb argues that transparency and accountability are essential in business, government, and other fields to prevent exploitation and maintain ethical standards.

The relationship between information asymmetry, morality, and responsibility is central to Taleb's framework. When those who make decisions on behalf of others lack skin in the game, the system becomes vulnerable to corruption and poor outcomes.

## Key Points

- Information asymmetry creates moral hazards without accountability
- Transparency and accountability are essential in governance and business
- Lack of skin in the game among decision-makers leads to exploitation
- Reforms to strengthen accountability within institutions are needed
- [[skin-in-the-game]] ensures decision-makers internalize consequences

